# tools/al/ales_stereo_smooth_trim_witness.rish -- stereo_smooth_trim: the smoothed silence trim carried into stereo, smooth each channel's runs before intersecting the two silences, so the joint strip declicks the pause and keeps the breath while the stereo image stays whole (ALES154).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES154.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-060518_fill-ales154-lotus-stereo-smooth-trim.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye (ALES148 -- the RAW stereo silence trim, the SILENCE INTERSECTION and lockstep cut, the min-zero twin, imported to prove the min-zero law), lotus/stereo_smooth_runs.rye (ALES153 -- the stereo run-length
# smoother, its smooth_runs_stereo run once to smooth BOTH channels' runs, StereoSmoothError reused whole), lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147 -- the stereo segmenter over one shared frame grid, segment_stereo/StereoSegments/StereoSegmentError
# reused whole), lotus/smooth_trim.rye (ALES102 -- the mono smoothed trim, imported to prove the LOCKSTEP law that running it per channel would desync the pair), lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 -- the mono Segments and max_segments), lotus/pan.rye
# (ALES10 -- the StereoClip and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, its cut, max_clip, EditError reused whole), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 -- the .silent verdict smoothed and intersected).
#
# lotus/stereo_smooth_trim.rye segments both channels through ALES147, smooths EACH channel's runs through ALES153 with one shared min_run (read-only over the labels, the two channels smoothing apart), marks a frame jointly-silent only where it
# is silent in BOTH SMOOTHED channels, and cuts exactly those frames from both channels in lockstep, walking back to front. It is ALES148 reading ALES153's SMOOTHED labels instead of the raw ones -- inventing no new measurement and no new sample.
# The crux is the stereo smooth-trim law: the result equals a by-hand keep of the frames NOT jointly-SMOOTHED-silent, per channel, across many frame lengths, floors, splits, and min_run values, both channels leaving equal length. Two seams give it
# its honesty: min_run 0 equals ALES148's stereo_trim_silence byte for byte on both, and running the mono smooth_trim per channel would DESYNC the pair (left to 8, right to 12) where this holds both at 12.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_smooth_trim_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: Language EN — stereo_smooth_trim removes every JOINTLY-silent frame from both channels of a stereo master AFTER smoothing each channel's segmentation, so a short voiced tick that smooths to silent on both is stripped with the pause, a short silent gap that smooths to voiced on either is kept, and the stereo image stays balanced. It segments both channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, smooths each channel's runs through ALES153 with one shared min_run, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in BOTH smoothed channels, and cuts exactly those frames from both channels through the timeline's cut, walking back to front so every earlier frame's index stays valid. A frame silent in only one smoothed channel is KEPT — the other speaker's content survives; only a moment where neither speaker carries smoothed sound is dead air, and only dead air is removed. min_run zero is exactly the raw stereo trim; an empty clip refuses BadFrame."
say "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: Style Radiant — ALES102 named the mono smooth_trim: segment a clip, smooth its runs with min_run, then strip the SMOOTHED silence, one knob that turns a literal silence remover into a speech-aware one — a short voiced tick buried in a pause is relabeled silent and stripped, a short silent gap inside speech is relabeled voiced and kept. ALES148 carried the RAW silence trim into stereo through the SILENCE INTERSECTION: cut only a frame silent in BOTH channels, so the two speakers leave equal length and the image never tears. This rung fuses the two — it smooths the raw segmentation of each channel first, read-only over the labels, then intersects the SMOOTHED silences and cuts the joint dead air in lockstep. The rule, stated once: segment both channels, smooth each channel's runs with the one shared min_run, mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both smoothed channels, and cut those frames from both channels back to front. ALES101's coverage law guarantees each smoothed run list tiles its channel's span exactly, so every jointly-silent frame is a valid span in both original clips, cut exactly as ALES148 cuts. The crux is that this rung is ALES148 reading ALES153's smoothed labels instead of the raw ones: it invents no new measurement and no new sample, reading its silences through ALES147, rewriting them through ALES153 which composes ALES101 twice, and removing whole frames through ALES2's cut. What is new is only the fusion, and its whole honesty is two seams — min_run zero is exactly ALES148, and running the proven mono smooth_trim per channel would desync the pair where this cuts once for both. Not monotone in min_run, the honest twin of ALES102's own non-monotonicity, now across two independent silences fused into one joint cut."
say "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) trimmed by removing whole jointly-smoothed-silent frames through the timeline's own cut on both channels in lockstep; the smoothing it adds is read-only over the labels, touching no PCM on either speaker. The two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (both lose exactly the joint smoothed silence, the length asserted identical on exit); a joint-silence mask sized max_frames equal to max_clip bounds the frame count, asserted at least one and within the mask; each per-channel cursor is asserted to name the smoothed run that actually covers each frame start; the jointly-silent total is asserted never to exceed the original length; StereoSmoothTrimError is the stereo segmenter's StereoSegmentError, the smoother's StereoSmoothError (the honest unreachable SegmentsFull), and the editor's EditError reused whole, inventing no fault of its own. The crux is proven against a by-hand second implementation (segment both channels, smooth each, keep every frame not silent in both smoothed channels) across five frame lengths, four floors, three splits, and six min_run values, both channels leaving equal length; the min-zero law is proven against ALES148's stereo_trim_silence directly (byte for byte on both channels across the same frame/floor/split grid); the declick law proves a short voiced tick jointly buried in silence is removed on both at a min_run past the tick, strictly shorter than the min-zero trim; the keep-gap law proves a short silent gap jointly between speech is kept on both at a min_run past the gap, strictly longer; the lockstep law proves the mono smooth_trim per channel desyncs to 8 and 12 while the stereo smoothed trim holds both at 12; the degenerate laws prove all-joint-silent empties both and a master loud on at least one channel per frame is unchanged on both whatever the min_run; and the atomicity laws prove an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name (BadFrame, BadFrame, BadThreshold, BadFloor) before any cut, both channels untouched and still balanced; siloed to lotus/, a causal left-to-right hold per channel, no adaptive VAD, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_smooth_trim"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_smooth_trim.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_smooth_trim.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-smooth-trim' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-smooth-trim: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-smooth-trim line"
